I've never read any Stephen King. For reading, I don't do novels, I prefer non-fiction.I do novels via movies.I really liked this movie, but I read a few 10 star reviews, (and a few negative reviews) and I was pretty incredulous about how much intense gushing went on about this movie, as well as some very interesting philosophies written out & even waxing poetic. It seemed the mid to low stars were given just tryin' to add balance, lol.Allegedly, The Godfather was once the number one movie of all time . . . ? (currently ranks 2 these days) I was a kid when that came out, and all I really remembered about it was that horse head. So I recently rewatched all 3 Godfather movies - and tried to consider the era/technique, etc they were produced in - but I gotta say - I was unimpressed. The Godfather was just 'ok' to me. It wasn't that compelling to me. I've much more enjoyed the endless parodies of it over the decades.Shawshank is a different animal - I'll just briefly touch on what I like;
Cast, direction, sets, & production, are all excellent. The fact that it's set in the late 40's, aside from just feeling like a classic movie, also gives credence to pulling off a carefully calculated planned escape - but the brilliant thing is, they captured the fact that much has stood still - very little 'updating' occurs in prison life, and that element is portrayed visually & it 'felt' still stuck in the 30s, even the 20s.Some noted the prison 'cliches' - well, these 'cliches' ARE prison life. That's the picture. Nothing changes. What would you expect?One cannot help but like the intended and compelling meaning of the story being played out which is this;
the tables of justice turned on those who abused the wielding of justice - the tables turned on the power-drunk who twisted & perverted justice, and knowing their own guilt, tried to hide it, feared their exposure.It's a running theme - I think the judge & the lawyers in the courtroom identified with Andy - but their fear condenmed him, they wielded their power to cover their own similar thoughts & fears. Of course, 'the sister's represented another kind of fear that must be overcome with power. The abusive guard also, and the warden being the biggest victim of his own crime, & fear of being found out.Prison guards, even wardens, are basically doin' time too. Here we get to see some coping mechanisms of good & bad guards, which explore the concept that many of the free men portrayed, as a primary example, captain Hadley, are as much criminals as any inmate.And this movie creates the questions; How does it get to be like this? How are some criminals free & their crimes are 'ok' or easily hidden because in their freedom they can abuse the power of their position of authority?The fact that Andy, is innocent, and WANTS the truth exposed, is the juxtaposition, AND uses his powers of intelligence & patience, to improve his position, and expose THE paradox of criminality is what is so compelling about this movie, combined with the fact that all this was done/told/portrayed on screen so well.Bottom line; this is an interesting, immersive movie portraying life inside bars, and what some of those lives looked like outside bars, whether free or incarcerated.This is definitely a keeper movie.If you have it on DVD you'll enjoy the special features too.

A film that got a lot of publicity;
The action takes place in space; the subject is realistic; the special effects are very impressive... But it remains a classic Hollywood scenario with happy ending, cutesy twists, right-thinking Judeo-Christian characters; Christian morality...in short, all of this is of poor quality.A film that got a lot of publicity;
